DEEP = [
T("Filtering logs with a generator",
  "A generator can sit between a data source and the code that uses it, passing along only what matters. Because it yields one line at a time, it works the same on a 10-line list or a 10 GB log file.",
  "def errors(lines):\n    for line in lines:\n        if 'ERROR' in line:\n            yield line\n\nlog = ['INFO boot', 'ERROR disk', 'WARN cpu']\nfor line in errors(log):\n    print(line)        # ERROR disk",
  "Each `yield` hands one value to the loop and pauses the function. The next loop pass resumes it right after the yield.",
  "Filtering logs, scan output or event streams before counting or alerting on them."),
C("Pull out the errors",
  "Finish `errors(lines)` so it yields only the lines containing 'ERROR'. It should print: ['ERROR disk', 'ERROR net']",
  "def errors(lines):\n    # TODO: yield lines that contain 'ERROR'\n    pass\n\nlog = ['INFO boot', 'ERROR disk', 'WARN cpu', 'ERROR net']\nprint(list(errors(log)))",
  "def errors(lines):\n    for line in lines:\n        if 'ERROR' in line:\n            yield line\n\nlog = ['INFO boot', 'ERROR disk', 'WARN cpu', 'ERROR net']\nprint(list(errors(log)))",
  "for line in lines:\n    if 'ERROR' in line:\n        yield line",
  [("Loop and test", "Go through `lines` with a for loop and check `'ERROR' in line` for each.",
    "print('ERROR' in 'ERROR disk', 'ERROR' in 'INFO boot')  # True False"),
   ("yield instead of append", "Where a normal function would append to a list, a generator writes `yield line`. `list(...)` at the bottom collects what it yields.",
    "def odds(nums):\n    for n in nums:\n        if n % 2:\n            yield n\n\nprint(list(odds([1, 2, 3])))")]),
T("Pipelines: generators feeding generators",
  "Generators chain: one reads lines, the next parses them, the next filters. Nothing runs until the final loop asks for a value, and then each item flows through every stage before the next item starts. Memory stays tiny however long the data is.",
  "def read(lines):\n    for line in lines:\n        yield line.strip()\n\ndef parse(lines):\n    for line in lines:\n        level, message = line.split(' ', 1)\n        yield level, message\n\nraw = ['ERROR disk full\\n', 'INFO ok\\n']\nfor level, message in parse(read(raw)):\n    print(level, '->', message)",
  "Calling a generator function only builds a paused generator object. Its body starts on the first `next()`, which the for loop makes.",
  "Log processing tools are built exactly like this: small stages you can test and recombine."),
Q("When does the code inside a generator function start running?", ["When the first value is requested with next() or a loop", "As soon as the function is called", "When the generator is garbage-collected"], "When the first value is requested with next() or a loop",
  "Calling it returns a generator object without running any of the body."),
T("itertools: tools for iterators",
  "The `itertools` module has building blocks for generators. `count(start)` counts up forever, `islice(it, n)` takes the first n items from any iterator (even an endless one), and `chain(a, b)` walks one iterable after another.",
  "from itertools import count, islice, chain\n\nprint(list(islice(count(100), 3)))      # [100, 101, 102]\nprint(list(chain(['a', 'b'], ['c'])))     # ['a', 'b', 'c']",
  "These functions are themselves lazy: they produce items only as they're asked for, so `islice(count(100), 3)` stops after three.",
  "Generating IDs, reading the first N records of a feed, or treating several log files as one stream."),
C("Take the first three ticket IDs",
  "`ticket_ids(start)` yields IDs forever. Use `islice` to print a list of the first three, starting at 1001. It should print: ['INC-1001', 'INC-1002', 'INC-1003']",
  "from itertools import count, islice\n\ndef ticket_ids(start):\n    for n in count(start):\n        yield f'INC-{n}'\n\n# TODO: print the first three IDs\n",
  "from itertools import count, islice\n\ndef ticket_ids(start):\n    for n in count(start):\n        yield f'INC-{n}'\n\nprint(list(islice(ticket_ids(1001), 3)))",
  "print(list(islice(ticket_ids(1001), 3)))",
  [("Never list an endless generator", "`list(ticket_ids(1001))` would run forever. `islice(gen, 3)` stops after three items.",
    "from itertools import count, islice\nprint(list(islice(count(5), 2)))  # [5, 6]"),
   ("Wrap it in list() to print", "islice returns another lazy iterator. `list(...)` collects its three items so print can show them.",
    "from itertools import islice\nprint(list(islice('abcdef', 3)))")]),
T("A generator runs only once",
  "Once a generator has handed out every value, it's exhausted: looping over it again gives nothing, with no error. If you need the values twice, store them in a list first.",
  "failed = (line for line in ['FAIL ada', 'OK bob'] if 'FAIL' in line)\nprint(list(failed))   # ['FAIL ada']\nprint(list(failed))   # []: already used up\n\nsaved = [line for line in ['FAIL ada', 'OK bob'] if 'FAIL' in line]\nprint(len(saved), saved)   # a list can be read again",
  "A generator keeps its position. After the last value it stays at the end, so a second loop starts there and finds nothing.",
  "A common source of \"why is my report empty?\" bugs, and a favorite interview predict-the-output question."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["3 0", "3 3", "6 0"], "3 0",
  "The first `sum` uses up the generator. The second gets no values, and the sum of nothing is 0.",
  code="g = (x for x in [1, 2])\nprint(sum(g), sum(g))",
  feedback={"3 3": "A generator can be looped over only once. The second sum sees it already empty."}),
C("Count and list the failures",
  "The report should print how many logins failed, then who failed. But the second line prints [] because the generator is already used up. Make `failed` a list so it prints: 2, then ['ada', 'bob']",
  "log = ['FAIL ada', 'OK cy', 'FAIL bob']\nfailed = (line for line in log if line.startswith('FAIL'))\nprint(len(list(failed)))\nprint([line.split()[1] for line in failed])",
  "log = ['FAIL ada', 'OK cy', 'FAIL bob']\nfailed = [line for line in log if line.startswith('FAIL')]\nprint(len(failed))\nprint([line.split()[1] for line in failed])",
  "failed = [line for line in log if line.startswith('FAIL')]\nprint(len(failed))",
  [("Brackets make a list", "Change the round brackets to square ones: `[line for line in log if ...]` builds a list you can read as often as you like.",
    "nums = [n for n in range(3)]\nprint(len(nums), nums)"),
   ("Then simplify the count", "With a list, `len(failed)` works directly; `list(failed)` is no longer needed.",
    "items = ['a', 'b']\nprint(len(items))")]),
T("Memory: lists vs generators",
  "A list comprehension builds every item before you use any. A generator expression makes one at a time. For a million numbers the list holds a million values in memory; the generator holds one. `sys.getsizeof` shows the difference.",
  "import sys\n\nbig_list = [n for n in range(1_000_000)]\nbig_gen = (n for n in range(1_000_000))\nprint(sys.getsizeof(big_list) > 1_000_000)   # True: megabytes\nprint(sys.getsizeof(big_gen) < 1_000)        # True: a couple hundred bytes",
  "The generator stores only its paused position and local variables, not the results.",
  "Totals and counts over big data: `sum(x for x in ...)` instead of `sum([x for x in ...])`."),
Q("Which line totals a huge sequence using constant memory?", ["`sum(x * 2 for x in data)`", "`sum([x * 2 for x in data])`", "Both use the same memory"], "`sum(x * 2 for x in data)`",
  "The brackets build the whole list first. Without them, sum pulls one value at a time."),
T("More from zip",
  "`zip` stops at the shortest input. `dict(zip(keys, values))` pairs a header row with a data row, and `zip(*rows)` turns rows into columns.",
  "headers = ['host', 'os']\nrow = ['web-01', 'linux']\nprint(dict(zip(headers, row)))     # {'host': 'web-01', 'os': 'linux'}\n\nrows = [(1, 'a'), (2, 'b')]\nprint(list(zip(*rows)))            # [(1, 2), ('a', 'b')]",
  "zip is lazy: it pulls one item from each input per step and stops as soon as any input runs out. `*rows` passes each row as a separate argument.",
  "Turning CSV-style lines into records, and pairing hostnames with scan results."),
C("Build a device record",
  "Use `zip` to pair `headers` with the values in `line`, and print the result as a dictionary. It should print: {'host': 'web-01', 'os': 'linux', 'owner': 'ops'}",
  "headers = ['host', 'os', 'owner']\nline = 'web-01,linux,ops'\n# TODO: print a dict pairing each header with its value\n",
  "headers = ['host', 'os', 'owner']\nline = 'web-01,linux,ops'\nprint(dict(zip(headers, line.split(','))))",
  "print(dict(zip(headers, line.split(','))))",
  [("Split first", "`line.split(',')` gives `['web-01', 'linux', 'ops']`, one value per header.",
    "print('a,b'.split(','))"),
   ("zip, then dict", "`zip(headers, values)` makes (header, value) pairs, and `dict(...)` turns pairs into a dictionary.",
    "print(dict(zip(['x', 'y'], [1, 2])))")]),
Q("What does this code print?", ["[(1, 'a'), (2, 'b')]", "[(1, 'a'), (2, 'b'), (3, None)]", "It raises ValueError"], "[(1, 'a'), (2, 'b')]",
  "zip stops when the shortest input ('ab', two items) runs out. The 3 is silently dropped, which can hide missing data.",
  code="print(list(zip([1, 2, 3], 'ab')))"),
T("Policy checks with any and all",
  "`any(...)` is True if at least one value is True; `all(...)` is True only if every value is. With a generator expression they stop as soon as the answer is known. Password policies are a natural fit: does the password contain a digit? an uppercase letter? a symbol?",
  "pw = 'Summer2025!'\nprint(any(c.isdigit() for c in pw))        # True\nprint(any(c.isupper() for c in pw))        # True\nprint(any(not c.isalnum() for c in pw))    # True: '!' is a symbol\nprint(all(c.isalpha() for c in pw))        # False",
  "`any` stops at the first True and `all` stops at the first False, so long inputs are often decided early.",
  "Password and configuration policy checks, and validating every row of an import."),
C("Password policy checker",
  "Finish `strong(pw)`: True when the password is at least 12 characters and has a digit, an uppercase letter, a lowercase letter and a symbol (a character that isn't a letter or digit). It should print False, True, True for the three passwords.",
  "def strong(pw):\n    # TODO: length >= 12 and digit, upper, lower and symbol\n    pass\n\nfor pw in ['password123', 'Tr0ub4dor&3x', 'CorrectHorse9!']:\n    print(pw, strong(pw))",
  "def strong(pw):\n    return (\n        len(pw) >= 12\n        and any(c.isdigit() for c in pw)\n        and any(c.isupper() for c in pw)\n        and any(c.islower() for c in pw)\n        and any(not c.isalnum() for c in pw)\n    )\n\nfor pw in ['password123', 'Tr0ub4dor&3x', 'CorrectHorse9!']:\n    print(pw, strong(pw))",
  "return (\n    len(pw) >= 12\n    and any(c.isdigit() for c in pw)\n    and any(c.isupper() for c in pw)\n    and any(c.islower() for c in pw)\n    and any(not c.isalnum() for c in pw)\n)",
  [("One any() per rule", "Each character rule is `any(test for c in pw)`: `c.isdigit()`, `c.isupper()`, `c.islower()`, and `not c.isalnum()` for symbols.",
    "print(any(c.isdigit() for c in 'abc1'), any(c.isupper() for c in 'abc'))  # True False"),
   ("Join them with and", "Return the length check and all four any() calls joined with `and`. Brackets around the whole expression let it span several lines.",
    "x = 5\nok = (\n    x > 1\n    and x < 10\n)\nprint(ok)")]),
Q("What does this code print?", ["False True", "True False", "False False"], "False True",
  "`any` of nothing is False: there's no True value. `all` of nothing is True: there's no False value. Watch for empty inputs passing an all() check.",
  code="print(any([]), all([]))"),
]

